"The prognosis is influenced by several factors, including a preoperative PSA of 11.4 and the pathological findings of Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2) with a clinically T2 stage. Favorable elements include negative surgical margins, no seminal vesicle invasion, absence of lymphovascular invasion, and no documented nodal sampling. The lack of nodal status widens uncertainty regarding regional disease extent.\n\nInformation that would significantly revise the prognosis includes postoperative PSA levels and their subsequent trajectory over time. A known pattern of falling or stable PSA post-surgery is informative. Furthermore, obtaining definitive nodal status through adequate sampling would improve risk stratification. Given the current data, the prognostic assessment relies heavily on the combination of preoperative PSA and surgical pathology findings in the absence of nodal information. The fixed estimated horizon is 69.39 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 45.11–93.68 months; its coverage has not been validated."
